import ContentPage from "../layouts/ContentPage.astro";
import PullQuote from "../components/PullQuote.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---
<ContentPage
  title="Publish"
  active="publish"
  kicker="Boundary III · Publish"
  headline="You are reading the third boundary."
  stand="Nothing about the lab crossed into this page. That is the whole mechanism."
>
  <p>
    This page is an artifact of the boundary it describes, and the honest description is
    simpler than the one you might expect. There is no filter standing between the lab's
    data and this site, because no lab data is sent to it. Every word here was written by
    hand. The site is a human account of the architecture, not a rendering of the
    inventory that architecture is built around.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is a claim about the pipeline, and it should not be read as a stronger one. Nothing
    here can leak <em>by ingestion</em>: no automated path carries a hostname, an address or a
    credential from the lab into this build. It does not follow that nothing can leak at all.
    An author who types a real identifier into a page ships it, because the build compiles what
    it is given. Hand-authoring moved that risk from a mechanism to a person; the tripwire
    described below is what moves it back, and it gates every build that can reach the
    public: the pull-request check and the deploy. A local <code>npm run build</code> does
    not invoke it, so a hand-copied <code>dist/</code> is not covered — the gate is on the
    path that publishes, not on the act of compiling.
  </p>

  <h2>Nothing crossed, so nothing had to be caught</h2>

  <p>
    The lab's source of truth lives in <code>inventory.yaml</code> inside the
    <a href="/single-source-of-truth">Describe boundary</a>. That file holds every host,
    network, VLAN, service route, and known issue in the lab. It is not published here.
    It is not copied here with sensitive fields removed. It is not read by this site's
    build at all: no code path in this repository opens it, imports it, or receives
    anything derived from it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/explorer">explorer's</a> physical layer is the one place a reader might
    reasonably expect real lab data, and it is not. It is a curated, deliberately generic
    stand-in — invented aliases, no real hostnames, addresses, or identifiers — hand-placed
    to show the shape of the architecture rather than the contents of the rack. The source
    file says as much in its opening comment, so a future contributor cannot mistake it for
    generated output.
  </p>

  <PullQuote>No filter keeps the lab's secrets off this page. Nothing ever carried them toward it.</PullQuote>

  <h2>What the boundary enforces, and what it does not yet do</h2>

  <p>
    Hand-authoring is a narrow guarantee and a weak pipeline. It rules out one failure — no
    automated path carries lab data here — and rules out nothing else: it cannot stay current
    on its own, and it cannot stop an author from typing something real. Every fact on this
    site is a fact a human re-checked. Two mechanisms close those gaps, and it is worth being
    precise about which one is doing work here today: <strong>one runs on this build, the
    other is built elsewhere and is not connected to this site</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first is an <strong>allowlist projection</strong>, which now exists inside the
    Describe boundary and is <strong>not wired</strong> to this site. Each field in the
    inventory schema may carry a publication marker; marked fields are emitted and every
    other field produces no output at all — not an empty value, absent. The point of a
    projection rather than a redaction is that a redaction is a scrub that can be done
    wrong, while a projection only ever emits what is explicitly named. Adding a field to
    the inventory keeps it private by default until someone deliberately marks it
    otherwise: fail-closed, not fail-open.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second is an independent <strong>tripwire</strong> over the build output, and it
    runs here, on every build and every deploy. It scans the compiled output for anything
    secret-shaped — private-range addresses, internal hostnames, key material, credential
    assignments — and fails the build on a match, before anything ships. Its value is that
    it is not the projection, and neither one's authors can talk the other out of its
    verdict. <strong>That independence is not yet doing any work here.</strong> Because the
    projection is not wired to this site, only the tripwire stands between an author's
    mistake and the public: one mechanism has to miss, not two. The two-guard property
    arrives with the wiring, and this page will say so when it does.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="publish" label="Status of this boundary">
    <p>
      The tripwire is built and runs in this repository's CI and deploy workflows. The
      allowlist projection is built in the Describe repository, where it emits a committed
      public artifact, and it is <strong>not wired</strong> to this site: nothing carries
      that artifact here. The safety property this page claims still rests entirely on the
      fact that <em>no lab data is imported here at all</em> — which is airtight while it
      holds, and stops holding on the day someone wires the inventory in. The difference
      from before is that on that day both mechanisms already exist; only the wiring is
      missing, and wiring is a decision rather than a construction project.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Static by construction</h2>

  <p>
    What ships is a fully static build: compiled HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. No server, no
    database, no server-side rendering touching live data. There is no runtime connection to
    the lab, no webhook listening for state changes, no endpoint that reflects anything
    internal. The pipeline terminates at publication. It does not reach back.
  </p>

  <p>
    That property does not depend on the two mechanisms above and will survive them. Whatever
    eventually crosses this boundary crosses it once, at build time, in one direction, and
    arrives as inert text.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="publish" label="You are here">
    <p>
      This site is Boundary III. It exists because the other two boundaries work: the
      <a href="/single-source-of-truth">single source of truth</a> in Describe holds the
      facts, and <a href="/operate">Operate</a> acts on them under a ceiling it cannot
      exceed. The lab has considerably more state than what is described here. What is not
      described is not hidden behind this page. It was never sent to it. See the
      <a href="/colophon">colophon</a> for how the build pipeline is assembled.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</ContentPage>
